age of Black’s early f6 move, which is not a standard opening move and creates vulnerabilities.\n\nReason: **P: e4-e5** directly challenges Black’s position, immediately creating threats and potentially opening lines for White’s pieces. It pressures the f6 pawn, which is not well defended, and if Black captures exf6, White can recapture with the g1 knight or the f1 bishop, developing with gain of time. If Black avoids capturing, they may have to move the pawn (f5 or fxe5), both of which concede weaknesses. This move seizes the initiative early and sets the tone for an aggressive game.\n\nAnswer: P: e4-e5'}
