ss unless capturing. So maybe Black pawn on h5 that moved two squares last turn? Could be en passant eligible. But in P: g3-h4, they list it without specifying capture, but could be en passant capture. Valid move list says “P: g3-h4” (not “P: g3xh4”). Either way, it’s legal according to engine.\n\n**Evaluation:**  \nWhite is cramped—king on g2 maybe slightly exposed. Black has knights that could jump into our territory. Our pawn on d5 is blocked by Black pawn on d6, but we have pawn majority on kingside. Black king is on h8, relatively safe. We have rooks not connected and one rook (a1) still unmoved, maybe useful later.  \n\nMoves:  \n1. **K: g2-g1** – King moves back to g1, slightly safer, away from center. But it retreats and may block rook on f1’s path to kingside defense.  \n2. **K: g2-h1** – King goes to corner, very passive, potentially risky if Black attacks on that side.  \n3. **K: g2-h3** – King steps forward toward center/kingside, but maybe exposed to knight checks (Nf2+, Ng5+). But currently no immediate check threats. Could help support pawn advance later.  \n4. **P: g3-h4** – This may be a pawn push diagonal? Possibly en passant capture of a black pawn on h5? If so, it gains a pawn and opens file. If not capture, then it’s an odd diagonal move to empty square, not legal—so must be capture of something invisible in display? Or maybe h4 is occupied by black piece not shown? Valid move list suggests it’s legal. If it captures, we gain material and open g-file for rook.  \n\nGiven valid move list, “P: g3-h4” is likely an en passant capture because Black pawn on h5 moved two squares last turn (thus eligible). If so, it’s a free pawn and good. Otherwise, if it’s just moving to empty h4 diagonally, it’s illegal unless capturing, so possibly the board display is missing that Black pawn on h4.\n\n**Reason:**  \nAssuming “P: g3-h4” is legal, it likely captures a Black pawn (maybe invisible in the ASCII art). Capturing a pawn improves our pawn structure, gains material, and opens g-file for rook activity. This is better than aimless king moves. King moves don’t achieve much now—king safety isn’t urgent. Taking a pawn gives us a material advantage and initiative.\n\nTherefore, I’ll pick the pawn move.\n\n**Answer:** P: g3-h4"}
