-g7+, if Kf8, we can play R: e7-e8# mate! Yes! Because rook on e7 covers e8, knight covers f8, g7, h8. Let’s check:  \n\n- N: e8-g7+ Kf8  \n- R: e7-e8#  \n\nKing can’t go to f7 (blocked by knight on g7), can’t stay f8 (rook on e8 covers f8). So it’s checkmate. If Kh8 instead after Ng7+, then R: e7-e8 is not mate because Kh7 possible? Wait: Kh8, then R: e7-e8 is not check — no, rook e8 not checking king on h8. But we have N: g7-e6 or something? We need to check: Actually after Ng7+ Kh8, rook e8 is not check. But maybe we can do R: e7-f7 threatening mate? That’s not in same move.  \n\nSo maybe not forced mate in 2 but very strong attack.  \n\nBest immediate move: **N: e8-g7+** forces king to move and we can follow up with R: e7-e8 or R: e7-f7 with decisive attack.  \n\n**Final choice:** N: e8-g7.\n\n---\n\n**Answer:** N: e8-g7'}
