k d1 to king g8? Rook d1 sees king after pawn removal. That’s discovered check + rook captures pawn. Good.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe most forcing and winning sequence is **R: d7-d8** – it’s check, so Black must respond by moving king (Kh8/Kf8). Then white can play **N: g7-e8+** forking king and rook, winning the rook on d7, or possibly mating.  \nBut let\'s verify: after Rxd8+, K moves (Kh8 likely), then Knight to e8 forks king and rook, rook is lost next move. That wins material decisively.  \nAlternative **N: g7-e8** immediately also forks king and rook but rook can move with check? Actually: Nxe8? Not exactly, N to e8 is check to king, king moves, rook on d7 still hangs to knight. However rook on d7 can capture something? It\'s pinned by rook d1 vertically – yes! Because if rook d7 moves, rook d1 takes king? Wait rook d1 doesn’t see king directly unless pawn d6 is removed. Actually currently rook d1 and king g8 are not aligned – d-file is blocked by pawn d6 and rook d7. So rook d7 is not pinned yet. That means if we play N: g7-e8+, black can just play Rxd1+ (check!), we recapture with king, then knight takes rook. That still wins rook for knight (good trade).  \nBut better is to first check with rook: Rxd8+ forces king move, then knight forks. This avoids the rook giving check.  \n\nTherefore **R: d7-d8+** is strongest.  \n\n**Answer:** R: d7-d8'}
