rect check on Black king. Forces Kg6-h6 or Kg6-f5, both dangerous for Black. Opens discovered attack from White rook on f7 if king moves.  \n4. **R: f7-g7** — direct check, forces Black king to h6, then White has follow-ups like N: e6-g5# or R: g7-h7# soon.  \n\nBest immediate checkmate threat: **N: e6-g5** is a discovered check if the rook on f7 is giving check? Wait — rook is on f7, Black king is on g6, rook is not checking because f7 is not same row/col/diag as g6. So N: e6-g5 is not a discovered check; it’s a direct knight check. That’s even stronger — forces Black king to move, possibly into mate next move.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nN: e6-g5 attacks Black king (KBF1 on g6). Black’s only legal moves are:  \n- Kh6 (then R: f7-h7#)  \n- Kf5 (then R: f7-f5# if rook moves to f5 next move, but king could also go elsewhere).  \nBut Kh6 leads to immediate mate with rook to h7.  \nIf Kf5, then N: g5-e4+ (forking king and something) or R: f7-f5+ captures next move. So N: e6-g5 likely forces immediate checkmate or decisive material loss.  \n\nThus, **N: e6-g5** is the most forcing and winning move.  \n\n**Answer:** N: e6-g5'}
