e, but not urgent.  \n4. **K: g2-f3** — centralizes king, moves toward center, supports pawns.  \n\nReason:  \nSince we are material ahead, centralizing the knight to a strong outpost while possibly threatening future forks is good. d7 is a good square, from which it can go to e5 or c5. a5-a6 is also good, but maybe premature if we don't have king support. Kf3 is solid, but perhaps slightly passive while knight is not yet optimally placed. Given that Black's king is on f5, moving our knight to d7 attacks no immediate targets but improves piece coordination.  \n\nGiven the options, I think **N: b6-d7** is a strong, flexible move that improves piece activity and restricts Black’s possible movements.\n\nAnswer: N: b6-d7"}
