es immediate pressure on Black’s king. After Kh5, my knight can go to f6 threatening mate or more checks. If Black king moves to f5 instead, then Knight from d7 to e5+ forks king and maybe bishop later.  \n\n`P: h2-h4+` also gains space on the kingside and opens potential for rook if I had one (but I don’t). Still, forcing the king to move is valuable in an endgame. The pawn move itself is safe and doesn’t lose material.  \n\nConsidering material — I’m up a knight and pawns, so trading into a pawn endgame is winning. Forcing Black’s king away from the center and into potential traps increases winning chances fastest.  \n\n**Answer: P: h2-h4**"}
