ng it.  \n\n**Reason**:  \nThe knight on c5 attacks Black’s only pawn (d6) and also supports advancing White’s d-pawn after exchanges. If Black captures Nxd6? He can’t (N on c5, pawn on d6), but pawn can’t capture knight; instead knight threatens to win pawn. Actually after Nc5, Black’s d6 pawn is attacked once; Black can defend with king from h4? Not immediately. Also, if Black ignores, White can play d5 later and create a passed pawn. This increases winning chances in a knight vs bishop endgame with pawns.  \n\n**Answer**: N: d7-c5'}
