 knight moves likely N: c5-e4 or N: c5-e6 because they create threats against black’s king and knight.  \n\n3. **King moves:** King moves are mostly defensive (g2-f1, g1, h1, h2, f2), moving it away from center and making it less useful. King should ideally centralize in endgames, but none of the king moves here help with that immediately.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe most forcing and active move is **N: c5-e6**.  \n- Threatens the pawn at d6 and checks the black king’s space.  \n- Controls central squares and potentially supports d5 pawn push later.  \n- Puts pressure on black’s position, whereas pawn moves or passive knight moves give Black the initiative with his bishop+knight coordination.  \n\n**Answer:** N: c5-e6'}
