e want to reposition for opposition in another way, but reduces pressure.  \n\nBest is to move closer to the Black King to reduce his available squares and possibly drive him to the edge (a1 corner eventually). **d4-c3** does exactly that — it’s the move that minimizes the distance between kings (from 3 squares apart to 2 squares apart diagonally) and keeps the initiative.\n\n**Reason:**  \nIn a pure king endgame, to maximize winning chances (if Black errs), you should advance your king toward the opponent’s king to gain opposition and restrict space. Moving to c3 pressures the Black King at c1 along the file and leaves future options like Kc3-c2 or Kc3-d2 to further close in.  \n\n**Answer:** K: d4-c3'}
